r itself.' On maintenance: the in-house brake bay was closed for refurbishment 4 Aug–19 Sep. Workshop coordinator Dev Okafor booked brake services that fell due in that period with Northfield Motor Services or Calder Road Garage, whichever had a slot first. 'Both fitted the Tessaro pads we supplied and did the standard two-year fluid change off the same job sheet, so that's not it.' The fleet engineer checked three complaint vans on 26 Sep and found no fault. Okafor is on leave until November.\n- Meeting note, Priya Nandakumar, driver and union representative, 15 Oct (staff-account): 'This isn't about how we drive. Plenty of us have done Upper Wend, and we all take turns on it. What changed is the pads. Head office switched to Tessaro in July because they were cheaper, and since then you hear squealing everywhere. Cheap pads on steep hills, they cook. And now people are scared to report in case they get a warning.' She asked that drivers who reported faults not be disciplined. Asked which vans have which pads or where vans were serviced, she said she did not know.\n- Procurement change record PCR-0417 with fleet engineering note (change-record): PCR-0417, effective 1 Jul: front brake pads for all depots switched from the previous supplier to Tessaro Friction. Pads are fitted as vans fall due for them, not as a campaign. By 30 Sep, 81 of 140 vans (58%) had Tessaro pads. Fleet engineering note, 6 Oct: 'Noise/squeal reports fleet-wide: 5 in Apr–Jun, 21 in Jul–Sep, 17 of the 21 on Tessaro-fitted vans. Caution: Tessaro vans are by definition the ones with freshly fitted pads, and we have no noise data on fresh pads of the old brand. FleetCheck also replaced paper defect sheets from 1 Aug.'\n\nBRIEF:\nDon't discipline drivers or run retraining. The evidence points to contaminated brake fluid. Eleven vans were serviced at Northfield Motor Services using fluid from one old bulk drum with a missing cap. All 11 vans with complaints are among those. Two other vans, serviced fr